% ==================== ABSTRACT ======================
\chapter*{ABSTRACT}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Abstract}
\thispagestyle{plain}
\begin{spacing}{1.45}
Large language model pipelines for automated literature review chain several
generative stages whose output is consumed downstream and, finally, by people
making real decisions. In current systems citation grounding is treated as a
\emph{scored} property: a generated statement is checked against retrieved
evidence and assigned a confidence value, flagged, or routed for human review,
yet it is not structurally prevented from reaching the final output when that
check fails.
\par\vspace{6pt}
This project describes \textbf{Verify-or-Reject Grounding (VoRG)}, an
architectural pattern that treats citation grounding as a \emph{build-time
invariant}. Each pipeline stage either produces output whose type excludes
unverifiable content, because the stage is deterministic over verified inputs or
because an explicit check discards non-conforming output before it propagates, or
it raises a build error. The work contributes a formal statement of the pattern,
a linear-time certification algorithm, a reference implementation across three
subsystems of an eight-stage research-assistant pipeline, and a reproducible
evaluation. The most robust result is diagnostic: the stages protected by a
verify-or-reject gate produce no output contradicted by the reviewed corpus,
while research-gap detection, the one major unprotected stage, produces a
confirmed false claim that a corpus paper directly contradicts. This
gap-to-failure correspondence motivates extending the same discipline to every
generative stage of an agentic scientific-NLP pipeline.
\end{spacing}
\clearpage

\tableofcontents
\clearpage

% ============================================================================
\chapter{Introduction}

\section{Background}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Large language models are increasingly used to accelerate literature
        review by extracting claims from papers, detecting disagreements, and
        drafting synthesis narratives.
  \item A well documented failure mode is \emph{hallucination}, where a generated
        statement looks well supported but corresponds to nothing in the source.
  \item A closely related failure is citation level: the cited source may not say
        what the model claims, or may not exist at all.
  \item Existing mitigations rely on \emph{post-hoc verification}: a statement is
        checked against retrieved evidence and given a score, a flag, or a review
        queue entry.
\end{itemize}

\section{Problem With Scored Grounding}
\begin{itemize}
  \item The validation signal is appended to the output, not made a gate that
        controls it.
  \item A downstream stage, or a human, can ignore the score.
  \item An unverified or contradictory statement can therefore still reach the
        user, and the cost of a hallucinated citation is high when the output
        feeds a real submission.
\end{itemize}

\section{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A formal statement of Verify-or-Reject Grounding as a reusable
        architectural pattern, distinguished from scored grounding by its
        build-time rejection semantics.
  \item \textsc{VoRG-Certify}, a linear-time $O(n+M)$ narrative certification
        algorithm that removes the $O(n \cdot m)$ term implicit in a naive
        per-sentence evidence scan.
  \item A reference implementation applying the pattern to three subsystems of an
        eight-stage research-assistant pipeline, released with its evaluation
        harness.
  \item A reproducible evaluation that reports a gap-to-failure correspondence
        between which stages are gated and where empirical failures occur.
\end{enumerate}

% ============================================================================
\chapter{Problem Statement and Objectives}

\section{Problem Statement}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Build an automated literature-synthesis pipeline in which no unverifiable
        citation-bearing statement can reach the final output.
  \item Do this structurally, as a property of the pipeline, not as a score a
        later stage may skip.
\end{itemize}

\section{Objectives}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Define, precisely, the difference between scored grounding and
        verify-or-reject grounding.
  \item Design a certification step whose cost scales linearly with the size of
        the evidence table and the narrative, not with their product.
  \item Implement the pattern in three independent subsystems: evidence-grounded
        synthesis, contradiction detection, and journal-metadata reconciliation.
  \item Evaluate the gated pipeline against a matched scored-grounding variant
        and a naive single-prompt baseline on a reproducible, gold-annotated
        corpus.
  \item Identify, from the evaluation, where the pattern is load bearing and
        where its absence causes a concrete failure.
\end{enumerate}

% ============================================================================
\chapter{System Architecture}

\section{Pipeline Overview}
The reference system is a research-assistant pipeline with eight sequential
stages.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Topic expansion (LLM decomposes the topic into subtopics, methods,
        datasets, keywords).
  \item Multi-source paper discovery (arXiv, Semantic Scholar, CrossRef; DOI and
        fuzzy-title deduplication).
  \item Claim extraction (per paper, per claim).
  \item Claim normalisation (metric-name and unit canonicalisation; rule-based
        confidence adjustment).
  \item Contradiction detection (LLM judge on an 11-point Likert scale).
  \item Research-gap detection.
  \item Citation building (BibTeX, APA, IEEE, MLA).
  \item Persistence (JSON run state; a relational journal-quality schema).
\end{enumerate}

\section{The Three Verify-or-Reject Subsystems}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Evidence-grounded synthesis.} Every extracted field is checked
        as a verbatim or near-verbatim substring of the source before entering
        the evidence table. A deterministic synthesis matrix aggregates the
        table with no LLM call. A narrative verifier rejects any generated
        sentence whose citation key does not resolve to a verified row.
  \item \textbf{Contradiction detection.} Judgments below a likelihood threshold
        are discarded, not reported with low confidence.
  \item \textbf{Journal-metadata reconciliation.} A metric is reported only when
        a source explicitly provides it; conflicting sources are surfaced in a
        discrepancy field, never silently averaged.
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
 node distance=9mm and 12mm,
 box/.style={draw, rounded corners, minimum width=3.0cm, minimum height=9mm, align=center, font=\small},
 gate/.style={draw, rounded corners, minimum width=3.0cm, minimum height=9mm, align=center, font=\small, fill=gray!14, thick},
 arr/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]}, thick}]
\node[box] (evi) {Evidence Extraction\\ \footnotesize verbatim-checked};
\node[gate, right=of evi] (mat) {Synthesis Matrix\\ \footnotesize deterministic, no LLM};
\node[box, below=of mat] (nar) {Narrative Generation\\ \footnotesize LLM drafts prose};
\node[gate, left=of nar] (ver) {Narrative Verifier\\ \footnotesize reject if \texttt{cite} unresolved};
\draw[arr] (evi) to (mat);
\draw[arr] (mat) to (nar);
\draw[arr] (nar) to (ver);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The evidence-grounded synthesis sub-pipeline. Shaded boxes are hard
gates: output that cannot be verified is not produced (deterministic
