\chapter{Evaluation and Results}

\section{Setup}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Pilot topic: \emph{graph neural networks in drug discovery}. Frozen
        corpus of 10 papers from CrossRef; in the pilot snapshot only one paper
        carried a non-empty abstract, which is reported as a first-class
        limitation.
  \item Configurations: full pipeline (gate on), rule-based pattern-mode
        ablation, and a naive single-prompt baseline. Three repeated runs each.
  \item Gold annotations authored by independently correcting pipeline output,
        not by rubber-stamping it.
\end{itemize}

\section{Claim Extraction}
\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Claim-extraction performance on the pilot topic, mean and standard
deviation over three runs, against three gold claims. Numbers are underpowered at
this gold-set size and are not used to draw conclusions about extractor type.}
\label{tab:claims}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
\headrow \hd{Configuration} & \hd{Precision} & \hd{Recall} & \hd{F1} \\
\midrule
Full pipeline (verify-or-reject) & 0.294 $\pm$ 0.042 & 0.889 $\pm$ 0.192 & 0.442 $\pm$ 0.070 \\
Pattern-mode ablation & 0.439 $\pm$ 0.211 & 0.667 $\pm$ 0.000 & 0.510 $\pm$ 0.152 \\
Naive single-LLM baseline & 0.104 $\pm$ 0.006 & 0.333 $\pm$ 0.000 & 0.158 $\pm$ 0.007 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Both the full pipeline and the pattern-mode ablation clearly beat the
        naive baseline, so decomposing extraction into per-paper, per-claim
        stages helps regardless of the extractor.
  \item The pattern-mode point estimate is nominally higher, which is attributed
        to the small gold set of near-verbatim sentences and to the much higher
        pattern-mode variance.
\end{itemize}

\section{Contradiction Detection}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Across all nine runs the false-positive rate on the two hand-labelled
        negative pairs was 0.0.
  \item No gold-positive contradictions exist in this small corpus, so recall is
        undefined; this is a precision-only sanity check.
\end{itemize}

\section{Research-Gap Detection: A Negative Result}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Gap recall against two gold gaps was 0.0 in two of three full-pipeline
        runs and 0.5 in the third, mean 0.17. Precision at 3 was 0.0 in every
        run.
  \item One run's top-ranked gap claimed that ``no studies have applied
        graph-based generative models for de novo molecular generation'', which
        is directly contradicted by the corpus's own sixth paper, ``Molecular
        generative Graph Neural Networks for Drug Discovery''.
  \item The gap-detection stage is the one component that does not apply
        verify-or-reject grounding: a candidate gap is generated and ranked but
        never checked against the corpus it is judging.
\end{itemize}

\begin{keybox}
\textbf{Central finding.} The gated stages produced no output contradicted by
the reviewed corpus. The single ungated stage produced exactly one confirmed
false claim, contradicted by a paper already in that corpus. The location of the
failure lines up with the one architectural gap in the reference system. This
gap-to-failure correspondence, not the F1 numbers, is the strongest evidence for
build-time rejection over post-hoc scoring.
\end{keybox}

\section{Cost}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Mean cost per full-pipeline run was USD 0.008, about 16 LLM calls.
  \item The matched scored-grounding ablation issues the same calls, so it costs
        the same; the difference is in what the output type permits.
\end{itemize}

\section{Work in Progress}
\begin{itemize}
  \item A matched scored-grounding ablation on identical output, and a
        multi-topic corpus with an independent annotator, are implemented in the
        harness and pending gold annotation.
\end{itemize}

% ============================================================================
\chapter{Conclusion and Future Work}

\section{Conclusion}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Verify-or-Reject Grounding treats citation grounding as a build-time
        invariant rather than a scored property.
  \item It was implemented across three subsystems of an eight-stage pipeline and
        given a linear-time certification algorithm.
  \item The evaluation shows a clean correspondence between architectural
        coverage and failure location: gated stages did not fail against the
        corpus, the ungated stage did.
\end{itemize}

\section{Future Work}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Apply the verify-or-reject mechanism to research-gap detection, closing
        the one architectural gap identified here.
  \item Run the matched scored-grounding ablation and broaden the evaluation to
        three to five gold-annotated topics with an independent annotator.
  \item Submit the full paper to an IEEE Xplore indexed venue; the current
        primary target is ICACCS 2027.
\end{itemize}

